    "chunk_size_bytes": 879,
    "chunk_text": "Since the overriding priority for a country in the Global South such as Sri Lanka should be the creation of a productive economy, two Ricardian principles of taxation deserve particular attention. First, Ricardo argued that taxes on essential commodities should be kept to a minimum because they raise of the cost of living, increase wage costs, and ultimately reduce profits and in turn capital accumulation. In today\u2019s context, this would imply a zero or very low rate of VAT on essential goods. Such a policy would not only ease the burden on low-income households but also help contain inflationary pressures.\n\nSecond, Ricardo mentioned that luxury goods should bear relatively heavier taxation, since taxes on luxuries have far fewer adverse effects on production and economic growth.\n\n(The writer is a retired teacher of Political Economy at the University of Peradeniya)"
