    "chunk_size_bytes": 1845,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cAn EV is not simply another vehicle with a different engine. It is a technology platform on wheels. Therefore, servicing requires specialised knowledge, equipment and continuous training, an industry expert said.\n\nBYD\u2019s aftersales network has already completed more than 25,000 service and repair operations, while its service centres have the capacity to handle more than 150 vehicles daily across the island.\n\nThe four-storey purpose-built centre can service up to 325 vehicles per week and includes dedicated facilities such as a high-voltage battery room, EV charging infrastructure, advanced diagnostics, precision wheel alignment and customer-focused service areas.\n\n\u201cCustomers investing in new technology need reassurance. A world-class vehicle requires a world-class support system, a company spokesperson said.\n\nFurther strengthening its commitment, BYD Sri Lanka opened the Seeduwa Body & Paint and Accident Repair Centre in February 2026.\n\nThe facility, dedicated exclusively to BYD and DENZA vehicles, can accommodate more than 100 vehicles at a time and represents a significant step towards specialised EV accident repair capability in Sri Lanka.\n\nBy separating body repair operations from general servicing, the facility aims to reduce congestion and improve turnaround times for customers requiring accident-related repairs.\n\nMore than 33 certified technical staff have completed specialised training programmes, including overseas training in China and regional support programmes.\n\n\u201cThis transition requires people with new skills. Developing local technicians is essential for creating a sustainable EV industry,\u201d a company official said.\n\nThe company is also evaluating Sri Lanka as a potential technical training hub for South Asia, highlighting the country\u2019s increasing importance in the regional EV landscape."
