    "chunk_size_bytes": 1975,
    "chunk_text": "The university had workers from India, Pakistan, Bangladesh, and Sri Lanka. Many of them were Muslims, and they preferred traditional Arab attire\u2014robes and head coverings\u2014in place of the pants and shirts they wore at home. This they did, I was told, in order to curry favour with the Saudis.\n\nAs a routine, I would have my breakfast at home before leaving for work and would usually have lunch at the university cafeteria, where the food was very affordable. After finishing work, I would return home in the evening and later go back to the university cafeteria for dinner. On the days I went to the city, I got used to eating at Indian or Pakistani restaurants. Parents of some of my students owned restaurants in the city. Whenever I ate there, they would refuse to accept payment for the food.\n\nA Pakistani worker from my office had a young daughter who attended a school near my house. Every morning, his wife would drop the child at school and then come to my house to clean it and do the laundry. I paid her a monthly stipend for her services. Since my workload was light and I didn\u2019t have to focus on research as I had done in the US, I found myself with much free time. However, there were no entertainment activities to indulge in. So, I decided to restart something I had put aside for a long time: writing technical books.\n\nDuring my time in Saudi Arabia, I was able to visit Sri Lanka at least once a month, and often once a week. The related administrative process was time consuming because the university did not allow non-Arabs to leave the country without getting approval from them. I had to hand over my travel documents to the university office soon after I returned to Dhahran after a trip outside the country. Then, whenever I wanted to leave the country, I had to fill out a detailed application form and get approval from several officials, including the Head of the Department. Only then would I receive my travel documents from the university."
