    "chunk_text": "During my time in Saudi Arabia, I was able to visit Sri Lanka at least once a month, and often once a week. The related administrative process was time consuming because the university did not allow non-Arabs to leave the country without getting approval from them. I had to hand over my travel documents to the university office soon after I returned to Dhahran after a trip outside the country. Then, whenever I wanted to leave the country, I had to fill out a detailed application form and get approval from several officials, including the Head of the Department. Only then would I receive my travel documents from the university.\n\nDavid Da Cunha was an associate professor in our department in Dhahran. He was from the Goa in India and was a Catholic. He often mentioned that his ancestors came from Portugal and asked me whether mine did too. I said no, my ancestors had to change their names for the convenience of the rulers. David liked his smokes and drinks much like the Russians. On many a weekend he would fly to nearby island country of Bahrain, where liquor is legal for foreigners. Also, whenever there was a university vacation, he would visit places like Bangkok and Bombay to enjoy himself.\n\nThe crime was not the End: How social media can harm child abuse victims again\n\nLeave a Reply Cancel replyYour email address will not be published. Required fields are marked *Comment * Name *\n\nSave my name, email, and website in this browser for the next time I comment."
