    "chunk_text": "I was by now about to leave for Paris, and feeling seriously exhausted. Therefore, I thought it would be some relief if I could hop across to London from Paris after the conference and just rest and sleep for a couple of days before I got back home to attend to winding up operations and discharging my final responsibility of finishing the interviews. The press of work was such that peace was not possible to attain when one reached Sri Lanka. There was a meeting with the President about four or five days before my departure and since in any case she would have had to approve the number of days I would be out of the country, I thought of informing her personally about taking a couple of days off in London.\n\nChairing an interview board with the responsibility of interviewing over 200 senior officers for a limited number of posts in the Class I of a service is no easy matter. Once the structure of the interview is decided, which itself must be fair with proper balance and weightage given to all relevant factors, one cannot rush through the process. An interview cannot be conducted to suit the convenience of the interview board. One has to be fair to the candidates, put them at ease and give them a chance to talk about their achievements.\n\nIn the case of educationists the issue is further complicated by the fact that almost all appearing for the interview possessed post-graduate qualifications; some of them had written books; others had written numerous articles; some had won awards and prizes and so on. Everyone brings all these to show the interview board. Some are so heavily laden that they stagger in. Others have had to resort to the assistance of some of the office aides. Some of them bring in so much material that these have to be accommodated on two or three chairs in large heaps."
