    "md5": "b7b559b915d4b0d6df6881c2636b97c1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1703,
    "chunk_text": "By now I had less than two weeks to go in service. Strangely enough, I was not feeling any sense of loss or regret but one of relief. The government had not made any offer of any firm assignment with the exception of two telephone calls from the Foreign Secretary, Mr. Wilhelm Woutersz, which was more personal to the effect that I should now give my mind to going abroad as an Ambassador. Neither my wife nor I was interested in living in a foreign country for a number of years. What I was looking for was some assignment or assignments that did not confine me within four walls for eight hours.\n\nThis was my thinking, and it was on this basis that I accepted certain limited assignments in the private sector. The main features of these assignments were (1) Not full-time, in the sense of not having to be present in an office full-time; (2) No line or operational functions and no responsibilities for day to day decision making; (3) Responsibilities only for lateral advice in a staff capacity on relevant official policy issues; (4) A fair responsibility for human resource development; and (5) Assistance in trouble shooting as and when relevant and required. Some of this work was interesting and creative, particularly the human development side.\n\nThe major difference between working at senior levels in the private sector, as opposed to the public sector is that in the public sector everybody else manages your time. You don\u2019t. In the private sector you manage your time. Nobody else does. In the public sector, the disturbance factor is consistently very high, and often the merely urgent overtakes and overwhelms the important mostly in situations where the urgent is relatively trivial."
