    "md5": "f93ae2375ad7b296fba881948a2db33e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1996,
    "chunk_text": "Then a series of misfortunes and policy blunders hit the GR administration with disastrous results. Some were beyond his control while others were \u201cman made\u201d due to his lack of experience. The net result was that he had to leave office prematurely under humiliating conditions. It was a disaster of the highest magnitude and previously not seen in Sri Lankan politics.\n\nFrom 2006 to 2014 economic growth averaged 6.5 percent per year. Per capita GDP increased from USD 1,242 to USD 3,819 by 2014. The debt to GDP ratio had declined from 90 percent in 2005 to 69 percent in 2014. As the IMF country report of September 2014 stated \u201cSri Lanka\u2019s economic growth has been among the fastest in Asia\u2019s frontier and developing economies in recent years.\u201d We were no longer a LDC (least developed country) but a middle income country.\n\nNo doubt after the global recession governments were pumping liquidity into their economies to stimulate growth. Without investment the lagging industries and services which had taken a hit could not be resurrected to pre-crisis levels. During the US meltdown the government, on the recommendation of the Fed, had even reversed their policies regarding bailing out private entities when they pumped money into insolvent enterprises, particularly banks. They were, in the celebrated phrase of the time \u201cToo big to fail\u201d.\n\nIn Sri Lanka, instead of pumping money from the Treasury to ailing enterprises PBJ presented a novel idea. To short circuit bureaucratic delays which the private sector had always complained about [particularly about delays in VAT reimbursements and organized corruption in delaying payments] he slashed taxes from the big companies which meant that government revenue was greatly reduced with all its attendant consequences. To do this he roped in WD Lakshman, his nominee to head the Central Bank, who allowed the printing of money to cover part of the deficit in accordance with new monetary theories emanating from US academia."
