    "chunk_size_bytes": 1809,
    "chunk_text": "Pioneering the Introduction of Japanese-Style Management in Sri Lanka\n\nLESSONS FROM MY CAREER: SYNTHESISING MANAGEMENT THEORY WITH PRACTICE \u2013 PART 39\n\nSome experiences remain etched in one\u2019s memory for a lifetime. Mine occurred in Japan in 1980 when, as a young Industrial Engineer following a course in Industrial and Systems Engineering, I witnessed a Quality Circle in action for the very first time. Little did I realise then that this single factory visit would profoundly influence my professional life and eventually lead to the introduction of Japanese-style Quality Circles to Sri Lanka.\n\nOne of the factories we visited belonged to NEC. There, we were invited to observe a Quality Circle presentation conducted entirely by shop-floor workers. This was long before the days of PowerPoint presentations. Instead, the team used colourful transparencies on an overhead projector. Yet, despite the absence of modern technology, it remains one of the most captivating presentations I have ever witnessed.\n\nI was astonished by the enthusiasm, confidence and commitment displayed by these ordinary factory workers. Drawing entirely on their own practical experience and intimate knowledge of their work, they had followed a disciplined problem-solving process. They identified the root causes of a production issue, generated alternative solutions, evaluated each option carefully, selected the most appropriate remedy and presented it to management. Once approval was obtained, the circle itself implemented the solution and established new standards to ensure that the improvement would be sustained.\n\nWhat impressed me most was not merely the technical excellence of their work but the genuine enjoyment they derived from the entire process. The presentation itself was a masterpiece of storytelling."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
