    "chunk_size_bytes": 1966,
    "chunk_text": "Growing up as a Catholic boy in Hambantota\n\nMy father passed the English Trained Teachers\u2019 Examination with first-class honours in 1951. He soon secured an assistant teacher position at Holy Cross College, his alma mater, in Kalutara. He was happy to return to his school as an English and Mathematics teacher. Our family\u2019s income improved because of my father\u2019s promotion. My mother had two more sons\u2014Gamini in 1952, and Parakrama in 1954.\n\nIn 1953, my father left Holy Cross College to join Aluthgama Maha Vidyalaya as its deputy head \u2013 another promotion. In 1955, at the age of 35, he joined St. Mary\u2019s English College in Hambantota as its Principal. At that time, Hambantota was a small town known for its large salterns and beautiful beaches, about 140 miles southeast of Colombo.\n\nIn Hambantota, we lived in an old rented house with several large, poorly lit rooms. A large kohomba (margosa) tree spread its branches over the house, making the rooms darker. The garden was about one acre in size, with a few murunga trees and scrub. We had electricity for the first time in our lives but had to fetch water from a public tap about 50 yards from our home.\n\nThe toilet was a big hole in a concrete slab with a bucket under it and a toilet cleaner came early every morning. Across the road from us was the district Base Hospital. Next to the hospital were the church and the primary Sinhala school, and St Mary\u2019s College was just behind the parish priest\u2019s residence.\n\nI joined the lower kindergarten class at the primary Sinhala school. On the first day at school, I cried saying that I did not want to leave my mother at home. Maria Gurunnanse, an old teacher who had taught my mother at Paiyagala many years ago was on the teaching staff. She shouted over the school wall, saying that my mother should be punished for keeping me at home. Then I apparently told her not to punish my mother, and that I would come to school every day without her."
