    "chunk_text": "I joined the lower kindergarten class at the primary Sinhala school. On the first day at school, I cried saying that I did not want to leave my mother at home. Maria Gurunnanse, an old teacher who had taught my mother at Paiyagala many years ago was on the teaching staff. She shouted over the school wall, saying that my mother should be punished for keeping me at home. Then I apparently told her not to punish my mother, and that I would come to school every day without her.\n\nMiss Seetha and my mother smelled alike because they used cuticira talcum powder. I wanted to stay close to Miss Seetha, sometimes thinking of her as my mother. Miss Seetha taught us how to count from 1 to 20. It was easy for me to study numbers, as I already knew how to count up to 50.\n\nWhen the kindergarten class ended at 11.30, my mother was at the school gate to take me home. I told her about Miss Seetha while she fed me rice from her plate in the kitchen. That evening, I ran a mock class for our young house helper. I told her to repeat the numbers after me, and I had enough patience not to shout when she made a mistake. I tried to write a few numbers on my slate with my mother\u2019s help before dinner.\n\nThe following day, when I went to school, Miss Seetha saw my slate and praised me for doing a lot of homework. She ticked the bottom of the slate with chalk to mark that I had got my numbers right. I kept that tick until I went home that afternoon. My mother told me that Miss Seetha was very impressed with my knowledge and homework. Proud of me, my mother told me to study as my father had done. I, too, was proud of myself and decided to count to 100 soon and read the books my father had in his study.\n\nI was impatient to tell Miss Seetha that a few days later, I could count to 50. She kissed my forehead and stroked my head, saying, \u201cYou are a bright boy.\u201d"
