    "md5": "08994d2075395f638b52d935289be177",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1852,
    "chunk_text": "She inquired about my studies, sports engagements, parents, and friends. Sometimes she brought me a few toffees or biscuits. She told me that she was a nurse at the hospital. I told her my mother was recently ill and had received some medicine from the hospital. She told me that my mother should tell her if she needed any help at the hospital.\n\nAfter she left me at the gate, I felt lonely and wished she had stayed a little longer chatting with me. I remember the small handkerchief she carried in her hand and the lovely smell of \u2018Cuticura\u2019 talcum powder mixed into the scent of her sweat.\n\nAbout three months later, I saw Nilani Aunty waiting for me at our gate. I came home late that day after playing cricket in the church compound. She was pleased to see me and used her handkerchief to wipe sweat from my face after wiping her own face. She wanted to know more about my studies and plans. I told her that I wanted to become a doctor. She smiled and said, \u201cThen I will be your nurse. Study hard and become a good doctor.\u201d\n\nOne day, she brought me a small paper bag of biscuits. She told me she might not see me again because she was going to a faraway place. She stroked my head, kissed my cheeks, and told me to be a good boy and study well. She wanted one day to hear about me and my achievements.\n\nShe had tears in her eyes. Perhaps that was the only time I saw her face. I asked her where she was going and how to talk to her. She did not answer. I told her I would be sad and miss her.\n\nThen she left me and started walking away. I watched her slow movement. She walked without looking back. I remember watching her sorrowfully as she disappeared at the far end of the road. I had not told my mother about Nilani Aunty. But when grief overwhelmed me that evening, I told her about her gentleness and how sad I would be to miss her."
