    "chunk_size_bytes": 1886,
    "chunk_text": "I blamed my mother for not supporting me. In the end, the priest reluctantly agreed to hear my confession and include me in the list of children eligible to receive Holy Communion. Each eligible boy and girl dressed in white as a bridegroom or bride for the occasion. I remember my father teasing me with his friends about my confessions. They wanted to know what sins I had committed as a young brat!\n\nSoon after my first Holy Communion, my father\u2019s cousin, Uncle Julian, visited us in Hambantota. He wanted to learn how to fire a gun at a target. My father, Uncle Julian, and I went to a nearby saltern in front of the college grounds for shooting practice. I carried a tin full of number four cartridges and a large sheet of paper mounted on a light bamboo frame.\n\nMy father proudly carried his 12-bore Stevenson single-barreled gun and walked in front of us. Uncle Julian had an umbrella against the sun. He walked slowly because his bad knees were painful. While walking, he asked me how often I attend church services and catechism classes. I avoided his questions by joining my father.\n\nI felt proud to walk with him carrying the cartridge tin. We saw lots of birds at the saltern. My father set up the bamboo frame with a sheet of white paper and drew several concentric circles on the paper. He mounted it on the frame and kept it against a bush. Uncle Julian stood about 50 meters from the frame and fired a few shots on my father\u2019s instructions. After the third shot, he complained about the gun\u2019s recoil. After each shot was fired, I ran to the frame and checked the paper. It indeed had several pellet marks.\n\nUncle Julian was delighted, and on our return, my father killed two \u2018red legs\u2019 birds for dinner. We walked silently for a short distance on an unpaved, narrow path. Uncle Julian stopped and asked my father, \u201cYou do not go to church on Sundays, do you?"
