    "md5": "b454de7c2f6fd7ac999aad7768c66cfa",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1734,
    "chunk_text": "Why SL needs a National Talent and International Mobility Strategy\n\nFor more than five decades, Sri Lanka has been recognised internationally as a country that sends its people abroad. Labour migration has generated billions of dollars in foreign exchange, supported millions of families and made an invaluable contribution to the national economy. Successive governments have, therefore, focused considerable attention on promoting overseas employment and protecting Sri Lankan migrant workers.\n\nThe country is experiencing shortages of workers and specialised skills across many sectors of the economy. Hotels struggle to recruit experienced staff. The construction industry experiences delays due to labour shortages. Manufacturing companies report difficulties in finding skilled technicians. The information technology sector continues to seek highly qualified professionals, while universities, research institutions and healthcare providers face increasing challenges in attracting and retaining talent. At the same time, thousands of educated and experienced Sri Lankans continue to migrate overseas in search of better opportunities.\n\nCan Sri Lanka achieve its economic aspirations by focusing only on sending workers abroad, or must it also become a country capable of attracting global talent, skills, knowledge and investment?\n\nI believe the answer is clear. Sri Lanka must move beyond the narrow discussion of labour immigration and develop a comprehensive National Talent and International Mobility Strategy that supports national development while safeguarding national interests.\n\nLabour shortages are no longer isolated problems affecting a few industries. They have become a structural constraint on economic growth."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
