    "md5": "7c04ee0b9fb023f50537d55d009d008a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1820,
    "chunk_text": "Labour immigration is, therefore, only one component of a much broader national strategy for talent attraction and international mobility.\n\nWhile discussing international talent, Sri Lanka must also recognise one of its greatest strengths, the global Sri Lankan diaspora.\n\nFor too long, national discussions about the diaspora have focused mainly on remittances.\n\nThe greatest contribution the diaspora can make is not only financial. It is their knowledge, expertise, innovation, professional networks, investment capacity and global experience.\n\nMany members of the diaspora are willing to contribute to Sri Lanka if provided with structured, transparent and meaningful opportunities. Some may wish to mentor young entrepreneurs. Others may support research collaborations, provide technical advice, establish businesses, invest in emerging industries or participate in short-term assignments.\n\nSri Lanka should therefore develop a comprehensive Diaspora Talent Strategy supported by initiatives such as a National Skills Bank, an Idea Bank, professional exchange programmes and diaspora investment facilitation.\n\nOur global diaspora should be regarded as an extension of Sri Lanka\u2019s national talent pool.\n\nCountries that have successfully addressed labour shortages have not simply opened their borders.\n\nThey have developed carefully designed systems that balance economic needs with national security, social cohesion and protection of local workers.\n\n*\u00a0 Protect employment opportunities for citizens through labour market testing.\n\n*\u00a0 Introduce specialised visa categories for investors, researchers and highly skilled professionals.\n\nThese measures demonstrate that attracting international talent is not incompatible with protecting national interests. On the contrary, effective governance strengthens both."
