    "md5": "535e8fe238f4a24d0eb99de02b7ab7f7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1760,
    "chunk_text": "Finally, modern digital systems, including biometric identity verification, integrated visa processing, qualification verification and secure information sharing should underpin all aspects of talent mobility.\n\nCountries that succeed in the 21st century will not necessarily be those with the largest populations or the lowest labour costs. They will be those that create attractive environments for talented people to live, work, invest, innovate and collaborate.\n\nSri Lanka possesses many natural advantages. It has an educated workforce, strategic geographic location, strong democratic institutions, rich cultural heritage and recognised strengths in tourism,\n\neducation and professional services. Combined with a globally successful diaspora and a carefully designed talent attraction strategy, these advantages can position Sri Lanka as a regional hub for knowledge, innovation and international mobility.\n\nThe question is no longer whether Sri Lanka should engage with global talent. The real question is whether we can afford not to.\n\nThe time has come to move beyond a narrow discussion of labour immigration and embrace a broader national vision, one that recognizes talent, knowledge and international mobility as strategic assets for sustainable development.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s geographical proximity to India presents a unique opportunity that few countries enjoy. India has emerged as one of the world\u2019s leading sources of highly skilled professionals, entrepreneurs, researchers, technology specialists and innovators. As India\u2019s economy continues to expand and bilateral economic cooperation deepens, opportunities for the movement of professionals, researchers, investors and knowledge workers between the two countries are likely to increase."
  },
