    "md5": "72171ef681c90c83e3fd55bf8f9d0c1b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1899,
    "chunk_text": "Decarbonisation remains a strategic priority for SAGT, with targeted initiatives continuing to reduce greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions while improving operational efficiency. As a result of the sustained efforts across its operations, SAGT achieved a 21 percent reduction in GHG emission intensity against its 2019/20 baseline by end-2025/26, reflecting meaningful progress in its long-term decarbonisation pathway. This reduction has been primarily driven by targeted Scope 1 emissions reduction initiatives across the terminal operations.\n\nIn parallel, the company is accelerating its transition to electrified equipment through the introduction of two electric forklifts and an electric vehicle for safety inspections, alongside the plans to deploy 30 electric prime mover trucks to further reduce the fossil fuel dependency.\n\nComplementing these efforts, the company\u2019s 460 kW rooftop solar photovoltaic system is now fully operational, generating an average of 44,000 kWh per month and meeting approximately 5 percent of SAGT\u2019s electricity requirements, marking a tangible step towards reducing reliance on the national grid and reducing Scope 2 emissions.\n\nThe investment of four new twin-lift ship-to-shore (STS) cranes will increase the twin-lift capability from five to nine units over the coming years. While the increasing operational capacity, the new STS cranes are also expected to improve the energy efficiency per container move, contributing to lower Scope 2 emissions. Complementing these upgrades, the ongoing investments in automation and digitalisation, including terminal gate automation, partial quayside automation through Optical Character Recognition (OCR) technology and a Vehicle Booking System, will improve the truck turnaround times, reducing idling and congestion while enhancing the overall logistics efficiency, thereby contributing to lower the Scope 3 emissions."
