    "chunk_size_bytes": 1633,
    "chunk_text": "More on Saudi Arabia: ARAMCO and beyond\n\nIn earlier years, the main oil company in Dhahran\u2014ARAMCO\u2014was managed entirely by Americans. Even during my time in Saudi Arabia, most of the top executives there were Americans. They lived in a separate, town-like area in ARAMCO. It had stores selling things that were normally restricted in Saudi Arabia, like bacon. Although alcohol was not officially available, many people secretly obtained bottles of liquor or brewed their own at home.\n\nMy friend David (da Cunha from Goa) often visited his friends there, mostly on weekends, and drank his fill. A couple of times, he invited me to join him as well. At ARAMCO there were some Catholic women from India whom David befriended. That was how I first tasted a dish called \u201cPaya,\u201d made from the feet of sheep. However, it did not agree with my palate. I have seen some Arabs coming there to eat paya and drink. Later, David started brewing beer at home and offered some to me. It tasted like vinegar, and I refused to drink his beer again. He brewed something similar to vodka as well, and had it with orange juice. He gave me a small bottle of that liquor, which I could not finish in a year.\n\nOn one holiday, I planned a trip back to Sri Lanka, while David arranged to return to India. We flew from Dhahran to Bahrain and planned to get our connecting flights from there. But, true to form, Dhahran airport caused long delays, and by the time we landed in Bahrain, my flight to Colombo had departed. David suggested that I fly to Bombay (now Mumbai) with him, stay there overnight, and fly to Colombo on the following day. I agreed."
  },
