    "chunk_text": "That night, we stayed at the home of David\u2019s relative. Before going there, despite David\u2019s objection, I bought food and drink to take along for dinner. Next morning I got ready to go to the airport by taxi. In my pocket, I had two brand-new, gold rimmed Parker pens, which I planned to gift to my relatives in Sri Lanka. A young woman in the house asked for my pen to write down an address and I obliged. Later, her sister asked me for the other pen, and I could not refuse. After a while, since I did not get my pens back, I assumed that they had forgotten to return them. So I asked one girl for the pen and she told me that she couldn\u2019t find it. I gave up the hope of getting my pens back, left for the airport, and reached Colombo.\n\nIn Saudi Arabia, most laborers were from the Philippines, South Korea, Bangladesh, and Pakistan. Many domestic workers were from Sri Lanka, Pakistan, Bangladesh, and the Philippines. Office workers were mostly from India, Pakistan, and Sri Lanka, while skilled technical jobs were filled by Westerners, as well as educated Asians like Indians and Middle Easterners. However, almost all administrative positions were held by Saudi nationals.\n\nThe biggest challenge I faced while working with them was the language barrier. Apart from a few Arabic words like \u201cAs-salamu alaykum \u201c (May God be with You), \u201cInshallah\u201d (If God so wills), \u201cBukhra\u201d (Tomorrow), \u201cShukran\u201d (Thank you), and \u201cSayyara\u201d (Car), I didn\u2019t know Arabic. Back in the US, I had bought a set of cassette tapes to learn Arabic, but I had yet to start using them. Pakistanis often say Salam for Aslam Walekum Also, Jewish people say \u201cShalom\u201d as a way of greeting."
