    "chunk_text": "The biggest challenge I faced while working with them was the language barrier. Apart from a few Arabic words like \u201cAs-salamu alaykum \u201c (May God be with You), \u201cInshallah\u201d (If God so wills), \u201cBukhra\u201d (Tomorrow), \u201cShukran\u201d (Thank you), and \u201cSayyara\u201d (Car), I didn\u2019t know Arabic. Back in the US, I had bought a set of cassette tapes to learn Arabic, but I had yet to start using them. Pakistanis often say Salam for Aslam Walekum Also, Jewish people say \u201cShalom\u201d as a way of greeting.\n\nAfter some time, the professor came to the point, and told me that the domestic worker who lived in his house was Sri Lankan. He said she was troubled and couldn\u2019t understand English or Arabic. He asked if I could speak with her and help resolve her prolems. The woman, a Muslim, spoke Sinhala fluently. The moment I started talking to her, she fell at my feet, begging me to help her return to Sri Lanka. She even asked if I could let her stay at my house until she could leave.\n\nI explained that it was illegal and could cause trouble for all of us. Instead, I advised her to remain patient and wait until her contract ended, after which the employment agency would arrange her to return home. However, she revealed something shocking\u2014she said that the professor was forcing himself on her and that his wife would often beat her with a stick. On hearing this, I promised to help her. Later I warned the professor that if she continued to stay in his house, she would commit suicide, as she told me, which would create serious problems for everyone. Fearing the consequences, the professor sent the woman back to Sri Lanka within a few days."
