    "chunk_text": "I explained that it was illegal and could cause trouble for all of us. Instead, I advised her to remain patient and wait until her contract ended, after which the employment agency would arrange her to return home. However, she revealed something shocking\u2014she said that the professor was forcing himself on her and that his wife would often beat her with a stick. On hearing this, I promised to help her. Later I warned the professor that if she continued to stay in his house, she would commit suicide, as she told me, which would create serious problems for everyone. Fearing the consequences, the professor sent the woman back to Sri Lanka within a few days.\n\nNevertheless, many Arab students treated me with respect. They often invited me to their homes or took me to large restaurants in the city, where they hosted me lavishly. My Iranian (Persian) graduate students were generally more intelligent and hard working. But they were quite different from Arabic students in many respects.\n\nIn my free time in Dhahran, Saudi Arabia, I started writing two technical books. Before leaving CMU I planned to write those books, and signed contracts for them with the reputed publisher, Lexington Books (D. C. Heath), with its head office in Lexington, Massachusetts. Since there were no PCs, laptops, or tablets in those days, the process of preparing a manuscript was as follows. The author would prepare a hand-written manuscript including sketches. A skilled clerk would then type in the manuscript and a professional draftsman would prepare the artwork. The final manuscript would be then sent to the publisher, with whom an agreement had been signed."
