    "chunk_size_bytes": 1522,
    "chunk_text": "In my free time in Dhahran, Saudi Arabia, I started writing two technical books. Before leaving CMU I planned to write those books, and signed contracts for them with the reputed publisher, Lexington Books (D. C. Heath), with its head office in Lexington, Massachusetts. Since there were no PCs, laptops, or tablets in those days, the process of preparing a manuscript was as follows. The author would prepare a hand-written manuscript including sketches. A skilled clerk would then type in the manuscript and a professional draftsman would prepare the artwork. The final manuscript would be then sent to the publisher, with whom an agreement had been signed.\n\nThe two technical books were, Dynamic Testing and Seismic Qualification Practice, and Automated Transit Guideways: Analysis and Design. The material for the first book came from the consulting work that I did with Westinghouse in Pittsburgh, on the testing of equipment for nuclear power plants that Westinghouse manufactured. The second book concerned Automated Guideway Transit (AGT) systems. Much of the material in this book came from my PhD thesis. It was coauthored by my research supervisor at MIT, Professor David Wormley and me.\n\nIn AGT systems, the vehicles move on elevated guideways in existing transportation corridors. The analysis and design of AGT systems was comprehensively presented in this second book. My effort in producing these two technical books became successful, and the books are available in the market even now (see figure below)."
