    "chunk_size_bytes": 1777,
    "chunk_text": "Building a Society Where Ability, Not Connections, Determines Opportunity\n\nWhen Lee Kuan Yew transformed Singapore from a resource-poor island into one of the world\u2019s most successful economies, he consistently championed one principle above many others: meritocracy. His conviction was simple but profound\u2014a nation\u2019s prosperity depends on ensuring that the most capable people occupy positions where they can contribute most effectively.\n\nFor countries seeking economic recovery, good governance, and public trust, meritocracy is not an optional ideal; it is an essential foundation.\n\nYet meritocracy remains widely misunderstood. Many assume it means academic qualifications, examination results, or years of experience alone. In reality, merit has no universal definition. It can only be assessed against the requirements of a particular role.\n\nQualifications, technical competence, experience, judgement, integrity, leadership, and personal qualities matter only when they match the responsibilities of the position. A degree in music has little relevance to becoming an electrical engineer, just as a medical degree does not qualify someone to be a skilled carpenter. However, designing and crafting high-quality musical instruments may require expertise in both music and carpentry. Merit, therefore, is always role-specific.\n\nA genuine merit-based system begins with clearly defined job descriptions. Every position should specify the qualifications, technical competence, experience, communication skills, language proficiency, professional standards, and personal qualities required to perform the role successfully. Every qualification and claim of experience should be independently verifiable.\n\nThis principle applies equally to the public and private sectors."
  },
