    "language": "en",
    "md5": "a6c1880375121ccdd44c0c1fd9ce2813",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1830,
    "chunk_text": "A genuine merit-based system begins with clearly defined job descriptions. Every position should specify the qualifications, technical competence, experience, communication skills, language proficiency, professional standards, and personal qualities required to perform the role successfully. Every qualification and claim of experience should be independently verifiable.\n\nThis principle applies equally to the public and private sectors.\n\nAppointments should therefore be based on objective assessments against transparent, predetermined criteria.\n\nAt its core, meritocracy means that appointments, promotions, and opportunities are determined by competence, achievement, qualifications, skills, judgement, integrity, and demonstrated potential\u2014not by family background, wealth, ethnicity, religion, social status, political patronage, or personal connections.\n\nThis does not mean everyone is equally qualified for every position. It means everyone should have an equal opportunity to compete for positions for which they possess the required abilities. Without this distinction, meritocracy can easily be distorted to protect privilege rather than recognise genuine talent.\n\nThe greatest challenge to meritocracy is that people do not begin life with equal opportunities.\n\nAccess to quality education, language training, technology, mentoring, and professional networks often depends on family circumstances and socioeconomic background. As a result, societies sometimes mistake inherited advantage for genuine merit.\n\nThe solution is not to lower professional standards but to widen opportunities. Governments should invest in better schools, vocational education, language training, digital access, and lifelong learning so that talent from every community can flourish while maintaining the standards society depends upon."
  },
