    "md5": "947c0b11a1f7363434dc943a42ae60bd",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1868,
    "chunk_text": "The solution is not to lower professional standards but to widen opportunities. Governments should invest in better schools, vocational education, language training, digital access, and lifelong learning so that talent from every community can flourish while maintaining the standards society depends upon.\n\nOne practical solution is the establishment of a nationwide network of Skills Development Centres aligned with labour market needs. Working closely with employers, universities, technical colleges, and industry, these centres should identify skills shortages and provide targeted training, apprenticeships, professional certification, and reskilling programmes. Their purpose should be to bridge competence gaps, prepare individuals for employment, and ensure employers have access to a competent and fairly qualified workforce.\n\nEnglish-language proficiency provides a good example. In many professions, English is not a social advantage but an essential working skill, providing access to scientific knowledge, international collaboration, and professional communication. Where English is genuinely necessary, it should remain part of the selection criteria. The appropriate response to unequal access is to improve English education\u2014not to reduce professional standards.\n\nThe same principle applies to university admissions. Sri Lanka\u2019s district quota system and Z-score mechanism were introduced to improve educational equity. However, after decades of operation, these policies deserve an independent review to determine whether they continue to balance fairness with academic excellence or whether reforms are needed to ensure that talented students are not unfairly disadvantaged.\n\nA successful society achieves both objectives: expanding opportunity while ensuring that positions of responsibility are filled by those best equipped to perform them."
  },
