    "md5": "ed1bfb5bc0d368f98daf6afd51e5638b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1841,
    "chunk_text": "The same principle applies to university admissions. Sri Lanka\u2019s district quota system and Z-score mechanism were introduced to improve educational equity. However, after decades of operation, these policies deserve an independent review to determine whether they continue to balance fairness with academic excellence or whether reforms are needed to ensure that talented students are not unfairly disadvantaged.\n\nA successful society achieves both objectives: expanding opportunity while ensuring that positions of responsibility are filled by those best equipped to perform them.\n\nA graduate entering a bank with a degree in physics may eventually become an outstanding chief executive\u2014not because physics alone prepared them for the role, but because they acquired financial expertise, organisational knowledge, leadership skills, strategic thinking, and sound judgement throughout their career.\n\nAs responsibility increases, technical knowledge alone becomes insufficient. Leadership, integrity, communication, adaptability, emotional intelligence, and sound decision-making become equally important.\n\nThis explains why organisations often conduct international searches when recruiting senior executives. They seek individuals who have already demonstrated the ability to lead complex organisations, manage change, inspire teams, and make sound decisions under pressure.\n\nNeil Armstrong\u2019s selection to command the first Moon landing perfectly illustrates this principle. NASA selected him not only because he was an exceptional pilot, but because he combined engineering expertise, technical competence, calm judgement, discipline, emotional stability, and the ability to make critical decisions under extraordinary circumstances.\n\nMeritocracy should guide recruitment, promotion, leadership appointments, and succession planning."
