    "md5": "c24fe51fc32a9f82b98a3b325ddfc7c7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1960,
    "chunk_text": "Neil Armstrong\u2019s selection to command the first Moon landing perfectly illustrates this principle. NASA selected him not only because he was an exceptional pilot, but because he combined engineering expertise, technical competence, calm judgement, discipline, emotional stability, and the ability to make critical decisions under extraordinary circumstances.\n\nMeritocracy should guide recruitment, promotion, leadership appointments, and succession planning.\n\nConsider a bus driver. The appropriate licence and driving competence are essential. Equally important are professionalism, ethical conduct, responsibility, and concern for passenger safety because every journey places lives in the driver\u2019s hands. If maintaining the vehicle forms part of the role, mechanical competence should also be considered.\n\nThe principle is universal: define the job first, then appoint the individual who best meets its requirements.\n\nSometimes this requires recruiting internationally while simultaneously developing local talent. Singapore has successfully followed this approach for decades, attracting expertise from around the world while investing heavily in educating its own citizens.\n\nSri Lanka has occasionally demonstrated similar pragmatism. When the newly established Faculty of Nursing and Allied Health Sciences at the University of Peradeniya urgently required experienced nursing academics, it legitimately recruited a highly qualified lecturer from India with the approval of the immigration authorities. The appointment made a valuable contribution while local expertise continued to develop.\n\nThat is meritocracy in practice: selecting the best available person today while building the nation\u2019s capacity for tomorrow.\n\nUltimately, meritocracy means selecting the right person for the right job through transparent, objective, evidence-based assessment.\n\n\u00b7 Integrity, accountability, and independent oversight throughout recruitment and promotion."
  },
