    "md5": "678e001170350bdea026b9b9e80c87a9",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1830,
    "chunk_text": "SLIMFA MediaFest 2026 calls for trust-first journalism as AI reshapes news landscape\n\nIndian High Commissioner Santosh Jha calls for stronger India\u2013Sri Lanka media collaboration through journalist exchanges and institutional partnerships to counter misinformation and build people-centric narratives\n\nMass Media Minister Dr. Nalinda Jayatissa says AI should strengthen journalism through better investigation and verification, while governments must safeguard, not influence independent journalism\n\nSLIMFA President Nisthar Cassim highlights SLIMFA\u2019s emergence as a key platform fostering professional dialogue, training and knowledge-sharing between Sri Lankan and Indian media\n\nANI Chief Editor and Deputy CEO Smita Prakash warns in the AI era, verification is as important as reporting, urging journalists to \u201ctrust, but verify\u201d\n\nThe Asia Group Partner Ashok Malik notes journalism remains a vital pathway to leadership and public service, while its role in holding power accountable is more important than ever\n\nIndia Today Managing Editor Marya Shakil urges newsrooms to adapt to changing audience habits by matching the speed of digital creators without compromising editorial discipline and trust\n\nIn an increasingly fragmented digital world, top media leaders from Sri Lanka and India said journalism's future depends less on technological innovation, than on its ability to preserve public trust, credibility and adaptation in an era dominated by artificial intelligence (AI), misinformation and rapidly changing audience habits.\n\nSharing insights at MediaFest 2026, organised by the Sri Lanka\u2013India Media Friendship Association (SLIMFA) at the Taj Samudra, Colombo, they insisted that news organisations must adapt to technological disruption without sacrificing the principles that underpin public confidence."
  },
