    "md5": "c4716d146eddd6d4d2b4a292688db00e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1093,
    "chunk_text": "Born in Doha in January 1952, Sheikh Hamad graduated from the British Royal Military Academy at Sandhurst before becoming commander of Qatar\u2019s armed forces. He became heir apparent and defence minister in 1977, assumed power as emir on June 27, 1995, and handed over leadership to his heir apparent, Sheikh Tamim bin Hamad Al Thani, on June 25, 2013.\n\nThe peaceful, voluntary transfer of leadership was rare in the region.\n\nSheikh Hamad had wide-ranging visions for Qatar\u2019s role as a diplomatic broker. Over the years, its mediation was brought to bear on the conflict in Sudan\u2019s Darfur region, Lebanese factional feuding and the rift between the Palestinian groups Hamas and Fatah.\n\nBefore Sheikh Hamad\u2019s abdication, Qatar formally opened an office for the Taliban at the request of the United States, which set the stage for talks between Washington and the Afghan group that ultimately led to NATO and the US\u2019s chaotic withdrawal from Afghanistan in 2021.\n\nDuring his rule, Qatar was among the few countries to side with the popular uprisings that became known as the Arab Spring."
